            "source_quote": "ὅπως ἀβασανίστως τοῦ ζῆν τὸν βίον μεταλλάξαι δυνηθῇς",
            "english_quote": "so that you may be able, without torment, to exchange the life you are living.",
            "issue": "The English treats τοῦ ζῆν as an attributive description of τὸν βίον: “the life you are living.” Although that attachment can be defended, the note does not identify or explain it. Instead, it discusses only the possible senses of μεταλλάσσω τὸν βίον. A material alternative takes τοῦ ζῆν as the counterpart in an exchange, yielding “exchange your life for living”; another possible attachment takes τὸν βίον with ζῆν, making “living your life” the genitive infinitival expression from which departure is envisaged. These alternatives concern the actual construction and the content of Peter’s double-meaning promise, not merely interchangeable English glosses. The existing cure-versus-death note does not adequately disclose this syntactic choice.",
